### Part 2: What If Oracle Dies or Data Is Lost?\
\
Because SQLite is just a single file (`/var/www/gh310-pms/database/database.sqlite`), **backing it up is as simple as copying that file**. If Oracle crashes, burns, or deletes the instance, having that file means zero data loss.\
\
Here is the 2-layer safety net:\
\
#### 1. Automated Nightly Backups on the Server\
\
Set up a daily cron job that copies the database and keeps the last 7 days of history on the VM:\
\
1. Create a backup folder on the server:\
```bash\
sudo mkdir -p /var/backups/pms-db\
sudo chown -R ubuntu:ubuntu /var/backups/pms-db\
\
```\
\
\
2. Open cron tasks:\
```bash\
crontab -e\
\
```\
\
\
3. Add this line at the bottom (runs automatically every night at 3:00 AM):\
```bash\
0 3 * * * sqlite3 /var/www/gh310-pms/database/database.sqlite ".backup '/var/backups/pms-db/pms_$(date +\\%Y\\%m\\%d_\\%H\\%M\\%S).sqlite'"\
\
```\
\
\
*(Using `sqlite3 ... .backup` safely snapshots the database even while guests or staff are actively using it).*\
\
#### 2. One-Command Sync to Your Mac\
\
Backups stored on Oracle won't help if Oracle itself goes down. You should occasionally pull a copy straight to your Mac.\
\
On your **Mac terminal**, create a tiny alias or shell command:\
\
```bash\
# Add this to your ~/.zshrc on your Mac\
echo "alias pms-backup='scp -i ~/.ssh/oracle_gh310.key ubuntu@134.185.83.115:/var/www/gh310-pms/database/database.sqlite ~/Backups/gh310/pms_live_\\$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).sqlite'" >> ~/.zshrc\
source ~/.zshrc\
\
```\
\
Whenever you want an offline safety copy, simply type:\
\
```bash\
pms-backup\
\
```\
\
Your Mac downloads the live database in two seconds.\
\
